<h1>Introduction to HTML</h1>
<p>
HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is a markup language used to create and
structure web pages. It defines the content of a webpage using HTML
elements (tags), which are displayed in a web browser.
</p>

<hr>

<h1>Key Features of HTML</h1>

<h2>1. Simple and Easy to Learn</h2>

<p>
HTML has a simple and straightforward syntax based on text and elements.
Developers can quickly learn it and start creating web pages without
requiring programming language knowledge.
</p>

<h2>2. Platform Independent</h2>

<p>
HTML documents run on any operating system, including Windows, Linux,
macOS, and mobile devices.
</p>

<h2>3. Tag-Based Language</h2>

<p>
HTML uses predefined tags to define the structure of a webpage, including
headings, paragraphs, tables, images, fonts, and many other elements.
</p>

<h2>4. Hyperlink Support</h2>

<p>
HTML allows linking one page to another page using hyperlinks. Hyperlinks
enable smooth navigation between web pages, documents, and external
resources.
</p>

<h2>5. Multimedia Support</h2>

<p>
HTML supports embedded images, audio, video, SVG graphics, and other
multimedia elements directly into web pages without requiring external
plugins.
</p>

<h2>6. Forms and User Input</h2>

<p>
HTML provides form elements such as text input, buttons, checkboxes,
radio buttons, and many more to collect user information efficiently.
</p>

<h2>7. Integration with CSS and JavaScript</h2>

<p>
HTML works seamlessly with CSS for styling and JavaScript for adding
interactivity, making it the foundation of modern web development.
</p>

<hr>

